# NavLink — a Link that knows whether it is the page you are already on. The
# current item is the one thing a bare link cannot express and the one thing a
# navigation needs most: a menu that never marks where you are is a menu you have
# to read every time.
#
#     use NavLink("Docs", "/docs")
#
# THAT USED TO TAKE A THIRD ARGUMENT AND IT WAS THE LIBRARY'S WORST ONE. There was
# no expression for "the route being rendered", so `current: bool` was a value the
# host worked out and threaded in by hand — which meant a nav written once inside a
# shared `layout` could not mark itself at all, because a layout is the same nodes
# on every route. The README listed it as impossible. `route` landed, this facet
# reads it, and the common case has no third argument: `route == href` is the
# comparison every call site was writing anyway, and it is written once here.
#
# `route` is readable in a view and in a component rendered by one, and nowhere
# else — not in a derive, a policy or an action, which have no page in sight. That
# is exactly the scope a nav item lives in, so this facet costs nothing to use and
# there is no cell to keep in step.
#
# THE OVERRIDE SURVIVED, BECAUSE EXACT MATCH IS NOT THE ONLY ANSWER. A "Docs" tab
# should stay lit on `/docs/getting-started`, a profile tab is current when the
# handle in the route is yours, and neither is `route == href`. `NavLinkWhen` takes
# the condition instead of computing one:
#
#     use NavLinkWhen("Docs", "/docs", contains(route, "/docs"))
#     use NavLinkWhen("Profile", "/profile/" + handle, handle == actor)
#
# The second destination is CONCATENATED and that is not a style preference: a
# component argument is an expression, so the quoted-and-braced form is a literal
# containing braces. It used to compile and render `href="/profile/{handle}"`; the
# compiler refuses it now. `+` converts and joins; interpolation is a property of a
# node's text, and the node is inside `Link`. This example shipped in the wrong
# form and a real app copied it out of here.
#
# It is the same markup and the same rules — `NavLink` is one line on top of it —
# so a nav can mix the two without the marked item looking different depending on
# which one drew it. Exact match is the default because it is the answer that is
# right without being told anything; a prefix default would light "Home" on every
# page in the site, which is the failure that makes people stop trusting the mark.
#
# The destination is the route-expression form, so an item pointing at a route
# this app does not serve renders as its label and nothing else (see link.fct) —
# which is the right failure for a nav: a menu with a dead entry shows the entry
# without pretending it leads anywhere.
import "link.fct"
app FacetNav:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-navitem { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;
                    padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 9999px; border: none; background: none;
                    width: fit-content; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-navitem:hover { background: var(--x-hover, rgba(239,243,244,.06)); }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-navitem a.x-link { color: var(--fa-fg); }
        # The current item. Weight and full colour rather than a background block,
        # because the hover state is already a background block and two of them in
        # the same rail are indistinguishable while the pointer is moving.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-navitem-current { font-weight: 800; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-navitem-current a.x-link,
        [data-fa-mount] .x-navitem-current span.x-link { color: var(--fa-fg); }
    component NavLinkWhen(label: text, href: text, current: bool):
        if !current:
            row class "x-navitem":
                use Link(label, href)
        if current:
            row class "x-navitem x-navitem-current":
                use Link(label, href)
    component NavLink(label: text, href: text):
        use NavLinkWhen(label, href, route == href)
